(window.FREE_Q = window.FREE_Q || {})["jaiib"] = {"qs": [["Accounting & Financial Management for Bankers", "₹10,000 is deposited at the end of each year for 3 years at 10% compounded annually. The value at the end of year 3 is:", ["₹30,000", "₹33,100", "₹33,000", "₹36,410"], 1, "FV = 10,000 × (1.1² + 1.1 + 1) = 10,000 × 3.31 = ₹33,100. ₹36,410 assumes deposits at the start of each year (annuity due)."], ["Retail Banking & Wealth Management", "A bank sells mutual fund units to its customers as a distributor. In this role, the bank:", ["Earns commission and must follow distribution norms", "Guarantees the returns of the scheme", "Becomes the trustee of the mutual fund", "Holds the scheme's securities as custodian"], 0, "As a distributor the bank earns trail commission and must follow SEBI/AMFI norms, including suitability and disclosure. Returns are not guaranteed by the bank."], ["Accounting & Financial Management for Bankers", "Net working capital is defined as:", ["Current assets minus current liabilities", "Fixed assets minus long-term loans", "Total assets minus total liabilities", "Share capital plus reserves"], 0, "Net working capital measures short-term liquidity: the excess of current assets over current liabilities that is financed from long-term sources."], ["Accounting & Financial Management for Bankers", "Why is depreciation added back to net profit when estimating cash generated from operations?", ["It is a cash receipt from customers", "It is a tax refund", "It is a non-cash expense", "It is a capital receipt"], 2, "Depreciation reduces profit but involves no cash outflow in the year, so it is added back to profit to arrive at operating cash flow."], ["Accounting & Financial Management for Bankers", "Simple interest on ₹50,000 at 9% per annum for 146 days (365-day year) is:", ["₹4,500", "₹1,825", "₹1,800", "₹1,314"], 2, "SI = 50,000 × 9% × 146 / 365 = ₹1,800. ₹4,500 is interest for a full year."], ["Indian Economy & Indian Financial System", "Which regulator oversees commodity derivatives exchanges in India?", ["SEBI", "Forward Markets Commission", "Reserve Bank of India", "Ministry of Consumer Affairs"], 0, "The Forward Markets Commission was merged with SEBI in 2015, and SEBI now regulates commodity derivatives exchanges and brokers."], ["Principles & Practices of Banking", "A savings account with no customer-induced transactions for over two years is classified as:", ["Closed", "Inoperative", "NPA", "Frozen by court order"], 1, "RBI treats accounts with no customer-induced transactions for more than two years as inoperative (dormant). Balances remain the customer's and can be reactivated after KYC."], ["Retail Banking & Wealth Management", "A Key Fact Statement (KFS) for retail and MSME loans mainly helps the borrower by:", ["Guaranteeing loan approval within a day", "Removing the need for KYC", "Fixing the interest rate for life", "Disclosing the all-in cost (APR) and key terms before signing"], 3, "RBI mandates a KFS in a standard format showing the annual percentage rate, fees and charges and other key terms, so borrowers can compare loans before committing."], ["Retail Banking & Wealth Management", "A credit card holder pays only the 'minimum amount due' each month. The likely result is:", ["No interest charged at all", "Interest charged on the unpaid balance and loss of the interest-free period", "Automatic closure of the card", "A higher credit limit the next month"], 1, "Paying only the minimum avoids a late fee but interest is charged on the outstanding balance, and new purchases lose the interest-free period."], ["Principles & Practices of Banking", "Which RBI-permitted method allows banks to complete customer identification remotely using a live, consent-based video interaction?", ["Offline Aadhaar e-sign", "NEFT verification", "V-CIP", "Positive Pay"], 2, "Video-based Customer Identification Process lets regulated entities verify customers through a secure live video session, with consent, as an alternative to in-person verification."], ["Retail Banking & Wealth Management", "A home loan of ₹10 lakh at 9% per annum is repayable in 120 monthly instalments. The EMI is closest to:", ["₹12,668", "₹9,000", "₹15,833", "₹11,250"], 0, "EMI = P × r × (1 + r)^n / [(1 + r)^n − 1] with r = 0.75% and n = 120, giving about ₹12,668. ₹15,833 assumes flat interest on the full principal."], ["Indian Economy & Indian Financial System", "Under RBI's liquidity adjustment framework, the floor of the policy rate corridor is the:", ["Marginal Standing Facility rate", "Bank Rate", "Cash Reserve Ratio", "Standing Deposit Facility rate"], 3, "Since 2022 the SDF, which absorbs surplus liquidity without collateral, forms the floor of the corridor, while the MSF rate forms the ceiling around the repo rate."], ["Indian Economy & Indian Financial System", "In a simplified model where banks keep only the CRR of 4% as reserves, the maximum money multiplier is:", ["4", "25", "96", "0.04"], 1, "Money multiplier = 1 / reserve ratio = 1 / 0.04 = 25. In practice it is lower because of currency held by the public and excess reserves."], ["Accounting & Financial Management for Bankers", "Current assets are ₹8 lakh, of which inventory is ₹3 lakh. Current liabilities are ₹4 lakh. The quick ratio is:", ["2.00", "1.25", "0.75", "2.75"], 1, "Quick ratio = (current assets − inventory) / current liabilities = (8 − 3) / 4 = 1.25. The current ratio would be 2.0."], ["Retail Banking & Wealth Management", "Under RBI's digital lending guidelines, loan disbursals by regulated entities must generally be made:", ["Into the lending app's pool account", "In cash through agents", "Directly into the borrower's bank account", "Into the account of the loan service provider"], 2, "RBI requires disbursals and repayments to flow directly between the borrower's and the lender's accounts, without pass-through or pool accounts of third parties."], ["Indian Economy & Indian Financial System", "Nominal GDP grew by 11% in a year while the GDP deflator rose by 4%. Real GDP growth is closest to:", ["7.0%", "6.7%", "15.4%", "2.8%"], 1, "Real growth = (1.11 / 1.04) − 1 ≈ 6.7%. Simply subtracting gives 7%, a rough approximation. The deflator strips out price changes from nominal GDP."], ["Principles & Practices of Banking", "In a safe deposit locker facility, the relationship between the bank and the customer is that of:", ["Debtor and creditor", "Trustee and beneficiary", "Lessor and lessee", "Agent and principal"], 2, "The bank lets out the locker space to the customer, creating a lessor–lessee relationship. The bank does not know or hold the contents as a debtor would hold deposits."], ["Indian Economy & Indian Financial System", "Remittances sent home by Indians working abroad are recorded in India's balance of payments under the:", ["Capital account, as foreign direct investment", "Financial account, as portfolio flows", "Current account", "Errors and omissions"], 2, "Private transfers such as remittances are part of invisibles in the current account. FDI and portfolio flows belong to the capital/financial account."], ["Principles & Practices of Banking", "A customer has given a mandate to her son to operate her account. The mandate is automatically terminated when:", ["The customer dies", "The son changes his job", "The account balance falls below ₹10,000", "The bank changes the branch manager"], 0, "A mandate is an agency; it ends on the death, insanity or insolvency of the principal (the customer). Changes in the agent's job or the bank's staff do not end it."], ["Retail Banking & Wealth Management", "A borrower earns ₹80,000 a month and existing EMIs are ₹12,000. The bank caps total EMIs at 50% of income. The maximum new EMI is:", ["₹40,000", "₹52,000", "₹28,000", "₹34,000"], 2, "Allowed EMIs = 50% × 80,000 = ₹40,000. Less existing EMIs of ₹12,000 leaves ₹28,000 for the new loan."], ["Retail Banking & Wealth Management", "Credit scores issued by credit information companies in India typically range from:", ["0 to 100", "300 to 900", "1 to 10", "500 to 1,000"], 1, "Most CICs in India use a range of about 300 to 900, where higher scores indicate better credit history and lower risk."], ["Principles & Practices of Banking", "Under the Negotiable Instruments Act, 'payment in due course' requires payment to be made:", ["Only to the drawer of the cheque in person", "Within one week of the date of the cheque", "In cash, irrespective of the crossing", "As per the apparent tenor of the instrument, in good faith and without negligence"], 3, "Payment in due course protects the paying banker when it pays according to the apparent tenor, in good faith, without negligence, to the person in possession entitled to payment."], ["Retail Banking & Wealth Management", "Before recommending investment products to a customer, a bank's wealth manager should first:", ["Recommend the product with the highest commission", "Ask for the customer's net banking password", "Sell insurance to cover all risks", "Assess the customer's risk profile and goals"], 3, "Suitability starts with understanding goals, horizon and risk appetite. Commission-led selling causes mis-selling, and passwords must never be asked for."], ["Accounting & Financial Management for Bankers", "A firm has total debt of ₹60 lakh and shareholders' equity of ₹40 lakh. Its debt–equity ratio is:", ["0.67 : 1", "0.6 : 1", "2.5 : 1", "1.5 : 1"], 3, "Debt–equity = debt / equity = 60 / 40 = 1.5. 0.6 is debt divided by total capital, a different ratio."], ["Principles & Practices of Banking", "A cheque bearing the words 'Not Negotiable' between two parallel lines:", ["Cannot be transferred to anyone at all", "Must be paid in cash at the counter", "Can be collected only for a bank's own account", "Can be transferred, but the transferee gets no better title than the transferor"], 3, "'Not negotiable' crossing removes the negotiability feature: the cheque can still be transferred, but a transferee cannot get a better title than the person who transferred it."], ["Accounting & Financial Management for Bankers", "A quote 'EUR 1 = ₹95.20' in India is a direct quote. The equivalent indirect quote is about:", ["₹1 = EUR 95.20", "₹100 = EUR 1.52", "₹1 = EUR 0.952", "₹1 = EUR 0.0105"], 3, "An indirect quote states foreign currency per unit of home currency: 1 / 95.20 ≈ 0.0105 EUR per rupee."], ["Indian Economy & Indian Financial System", "If RBI lowers the policy repo rate, the most likely immediate effect is:", ["A rise in banks' cost of funds", "An increase in the CRR", "Higher yields on government bonds", "Lower borrowing costs in the economy"], 3, "A repo cut lowers the cost at which banks borrow from RBI, which typically passes into lower lending and deposit rates and lower bond yields."], ["Principles & Practices of Banking", "A loan classified as doubtful (up to one year in that category) has a secured portion of ₹40 lakh and an unsecured portion of ₹10 lakh. The provision required under IRAC norms is:", ["₹10 lakh", "₹20 lakh", "₹14 lakh", "₹50 lakh"], 1, "For D1, the unsecured portion needs 100% provision (₹10 lakh) and the secured portion 25% (₹10 lakh), giving ₹20 lakh in total."], ["Accounting & Financial Management for Bankers", "The present value of ₹1,00,000 receivable after 3 years at 10% compounded annually is closest to:", ["₹75,131", "₹70,000", "₹76,923", "₹73,000"], 0, "PV = 1,00,000 / (1.10)^3 = 1,00,000 / 1.331 = ₹75,131. Subtracting 30% simple interest gives ₹70,000, which is wrong."], ["Indian Economy & Indian Financial System", "Pradhan Mantri Jan Dhan Yojana is primarily aimed at:", ["Providing crop insurance to farmers", "Funding large infrastructure projects", "Universal access to basic bank accounts", "Recapitalising public sector banks"], 2, "PMJDY aims at financial inclusion by giving every household a basic savings account with RuPay card and access to insurance and credit facilities."], ["Principles & Practices of Banking", "A cash credit account is treated as an NPA when it remains 'out of order' for:", ["More than 90 days", "More than 30 days", "More than 180 days", "More than one year"], 0, "Under RBI's IRAC norms, an overdraft or cash credit account that is out of order (for example, balance above limit or no credits to cover interest) for over 90 days becomes an NPA."], ["Indian Economy & Indian Financial System", "The government's fiscal deficit is ₹16 lakh crore and interest payments are ₹11 lakh crore. The primary deficit is:", ["₹5 lakh crore", "₹27 lakh crore", "₹11 lakh crore", "₹16 lakh crore"], 0, "Primary deficit = fiscal deficit − interest payments = 16 − 11 = ₹5 lakh crore. It shows borrowing needed for spending other than past debt servicing."]]};
